    right shape. Costs a new ID-generation decision and, if it should survive a page reload, storage — which would push
    this past bounded.

  [1mMy recommendation: the correlation-ID reading[0m, but only if tracing attempts is what you're after. It's the one where a
  parameter is actually correct rather than redundant or misplaced. If what you want is "know which account logged in,"
  the server-returns-it option is the honest shape and I'd change the return value instead of the signature.

  One thing worth naming either way: [38;5;153mlogin[39m currently logs the identifier to the console, so whatever [38;5;153muserId[39m holds will
  end up in browser logs. Fine for an opaque ID, less so if it's an email or anything account-identifying.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Where does userId come from, given the form only collects username and password? → Client correlation ID [39m
     [38;5;246m(Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Correlation ID it is. One more thing decides whether this stays a bounded change.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether the ID lives for one submit or persists across page loads. Ephemeral is a [38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID()[39m on the
  line above the [38;5;153mlogin[39m call — self-contained, stays bounded. Persistent means [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, which is a storage concern
  the repo doesn't have yet, plus a privacy question (a durable client identifier is a tracking identifier), and it
  would push this to the architectural path with a spec.

  My recommendation is ephemeral: it gives you per-attempt tracing, which is what a correlation ID is for, and it's
  cheap to upgrade to persistent later if you find you need cross-session linkage. The reverse — walking back a durable
  identifier once it's shipped — is the expensive direction.
